    "- Best reconstruction vs best neighbours: not the same setup. The conv AE with latent 128 rebuilds best (test MSE 0.0052), about half the error of conv latent 32 (0.0104). It is also much better than the dense AE (0.0188). But conv latent 32 finds the best neighbours (precision@5 0.140, vs 0.130 for latent 128 and 0.129 for dense). A bigger code stores more of the photo’s layout and lighting, but that doesn’t help find the same material. This shows that rebuilding well and searching well are different goals.\n",
    "- Compared with random: precision@5 of 0.140 is about 1.8× the random baseline (1/13 ≈ 0.077). The latent space has learned something about materials without ever seeing a label, but on average fewer than 1 of the 5 matches is the right material. Search works best for painted, wallpaper and carpet (about 0.23–0.25). It is no better than chance for metal and plastic.\n",
    "- What the reconstructions keep and lose: they keep the overall colour, brightness and the rough layout of the patch (light and dark areas, large shapes). They lose all fine texture: brick joints, wood grain, tile edges and carpet fibres become smooth blurs. So the code describes “a brown, dark-on-the-left image” more than “wood”. This explains why search mixes up materials with similar colours, and why patches with the same grey padding get matched together.\n",
    "- Was the bigger model worth it?\n",
    "    - Dense vs conv 32: the conv 32 model is clearly better. It has 7× fewer parameters (0.86 M vs 6.3 M), lower error and better search. It takes about 3× longer to train (76 s vs 24 s), which still doesn’t matter at this size.\n",
    "    - Conv 32 vs conv 128: latent 128 costs 2.8× more parameters (2.4 M) for a better redraw, but slightly worse search. For our app, whose job is “find similar”, it isn’t worth it.\n",
