    "# 7. Conclusions (write these yourselves)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Key findings (from our full run: 12 epochs, 27,625 training patches, seed 42)**\n",
    "\n",
    "The table:\n",
    "- Convolutions beat dense layers. The conv AE with latent 32 halves the redraw error of the dense AE (test MSE 0.0104 vs 0.0188) with 7× fewer parameters (0.86 M vs 6.3 M).\n",
    "- A bigger code rebuilds better but doesn’t search better. Latent 128 halves the error again (0.0052), but its precision@5 drops slightly (0.130 vs 0.140).\n",
    "- We chose conv AE, latent 32: best search, smallest model (3.5 MB ONNX).\n",
    "- All curves level off by about epoch 8, and validation follows training, so there is no overfitting.\n",
    "\n",
    "Similarity search:\n",
    "- precision@5 is 0.140 against 0.077 for random: about 1.8× chance, but fewer than 1 in 5 matches is the right material.\n",
    "- Works best for even, coloured surfaces: painted (0.25), wallpaper (0.25), carpet (0.23), tile (0.18), wood (0.16).\n",
    "- Fails for metal (0.06) and plastic (0.07), both below random. Brick, ceramic and stone are barely above it.\n",
    "- In figure 04, many neighbours share the same grey padding block, not the same material.\n",
    "Unusual score:\n",
    "\n",
    "- ROC AUC 0.65, only a little better than chance. At the 95% threshold it catches 11% of unusual photos (recall 0.11, precision 0.61).\n",
    "- Sky slips through easily (2% flagged): it is smoother than most building materials.\n",
    "- Hair, leather and fabric also look “normal” (about 8% flagged).\n",
    "- Food and paper stand out most (16–19%).\n",
    "- Glass, a building material, scores as high as water or foliage.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Limitations / where it fails:**\n",
    "\n",
    "Colour, not material. A pixel autoencoder groups images by colour, brightness and rough layout. The reconstructions are blurry colour blobs with no texture: no brick joints, no wood grain.\n",
    "Reflective and shiny materials. Metal, glass and mirror show their surroundings, and plastic comes in every colour, so their codes are scattered.\n",
    "MINC patches are scenes, not close-ups. Each patch shows a whole room or facade, and the label only describes the material at the centre. The model learns the scene, not the surface.\n",
    "Grey padding. Patches cut near the edge of the original photo have grey filled corners. The model matches on these blocks.\n",
    "The unusual score measures difficulty, not content. It can’t tell sky from paint, so the app must show it as a hint, not a verdict.\n",
    "Real user photos will differ again (phone cameras, lighting, angle). We haven’t tested our own photos yet.\n"
